          "body": "DIAG 42 Root cause of the persistent test_rst.py::test_write_normal failure is in RST.write, not in __init__: it hardcodes the position-line index as 1.\n\nEvidence (public source, my workspace v1): astropy/io/ascii/rst.py RST.write is\n    lines = super().write(lines)\n    lines = [lines[1]] + lines + [lines[1]]\n`super().write` is FixedWidthData.write, which emits one line per entry of self.header_rows IN ORDER, then the position line. So the position line sits at index len(header_rows)-... precisely at index len(header_rows) when header_rows has one element (\"name\"), i.e. index 1 — which is why the 1-header-row case works. As soon as header_rows=[\"name\",\"unit\"] the position line is at index 2 and `lines[1]` is the UNIT row. RST.write then duplicates the unit row as the top and bottom bookends instead of the \"=====\" separator, so the emitted table is malformed and test_write_normal's expected string does not match.\n\nFix: compute the index from the actual header_rows, e.g.\n    idx = len(self.header.header_rows)   # header_rows written first, then position line\n    lines = [lines[idx]] + lines + [lines[idx]]\n(guard header_rows == [] / empty by falling back to no bookend, which is what FixedWidthNoHeader does).\n\nTwo more things that will bite after that and are independent of the above:\n(a) RST.__init__(self) takes no kwargs, so core._get_writer -> Writer(**writer_kwargs) raises \"TypeError: RST.__init__() got an unexpected keyword argument 'header_rows'\" (this is the original issue traceback). It must accept header_rows and forward it: `def __init__(self, header_rows=None): super().__init__(delimiter_pad=None, bookend=False, header_rows=header_rows)`. FixedWidth.__init__ then stores it on both self.header and self.data.\n(b) Reader side: SimpleRSTHeader has position_line=0, start_line=1 and SimpleRSTData has start_line=3 hardcoded. Round-tripping a 2-header-row RST table needs those derived from len(header_rows) (position_line=0, data start = len(header_rows)+2). Mirror FixedWidthTwoLine.__init__, which does exactly `position_line = len(self.header.header_rows)` and `self.data.start_line = position_line + 1`.\n\nSuggested repair order: fix __init__ (a) first, since with it alone the TypeError becomes the wrong-bookend mismatch you are seeing — that is consistent with the same failing test on both v2 and v3.",
          "native_handoff": true
        }
      ],
      "recipient_disposition": {
        "choice": "ADOPT",
        "reason": "Your DIAG 42 is correct and I am adopting it as the fix basis. My v3 already replaced __init__ to forward header_rows (that removed the original TypeError), but my write() used idx = self.header.position_line, which is the READER attribute meaning \"the separator sits above header row 0\". In the WRITE path FixedWidthData.write emits all header rows first and then the position line, so the index must be len(self.header.header_rows). Your reading of the reproduced output (unit row duplicated as the top/bottom bookends, '====' pushed to index 2) matches exactly.\n\nApplying all three parts now:\n(a) __init__(self, header_rows=None) forwarding to FixedWidth.__init__ — kept from v3.\n(b) write(): idx = len(self.header.header_rows), and no bookend at all when header_rows is empty.\n(c) reader side: derive positions in RST.__init__ mirroring FixedWidthTwoLine — self.header.position_line = 0, self.header.start_line = 1, self.data.start_line = len(header_rows) + 2.\n\nNext: re-run astropy/io/ascii/tests/test_rst.py on the new version, then fix_patch and handoff_patch to you for open_patch_review -> run_patch_review_tests -> org_review_patch. Thank you — that diagnosis was the piece I was missing.",
